// A runaway recursion through a METHOD is named too. Roadmap B7.
//
// This fixture exists because the first version of B7's guard did not cover methods, and the suite
// went green anyway: `a_runaway_recursion_is_named.bx` recurses through a free function, and nothing
// in 617 fixtures recursed through a method. A guard over `function f()` and not `function (self) f()`
// is exactly the shape this codebase keeps paying for — a rule right about the case someone wrote
// and silent about the case nobody did.
//
// It surfaced from outside the suite, which is the only place it could have: stage-1's parser is
// recursive descent written as methods, so a 30,000-deep expression segfaulted stage-1 while
// stage-0 parsed the same file without complaint. One `bt` in gdb showed
// `parse_primary -> parse_expr -> parse_primary` with no guard between the frames.
//
// So the fixture is the free-function one with the recursion moved onto a receiver, and nothing
// else changed. Two places a user-written body begins; both must guard.
class Counter { seen: Int }

function (self: Counter) down(n: Int) -> Int {
    if n <= 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1 + self.down(n - 1);
}

let c: Counter = Counter { seen: 0 };
print(c.down(100000000));
